 { Black to move. Can he draw ? } 1... Kc5 $3 { Yes! This is the only move that secures the draw. Preventing the cut along the 3rd rank (which would occur after the pawn's advance) is not enough. The White King must be hindered as well, as analysis proves: } ( 1... a5 $4 { Loses on the spot due to } 2. Rh5 $1 ) 
 ( 1... Kb5 $2 { Allowing the White King to arrive } 2. Kf7 a5 3. Ke6 a4 4. Kd5 $1 { Done ! The White King shoulders his Black counterpart before the pawn reaches the 6th rank. Therefore, White wins: } Kb4 ( 4... a3 5. Rb8 $18 Ka4 6. Kc4 ) 
5. Kd4 $1 { Again. } Kb3 ( 5... a3 6. Rb8 $18 ) 
6. Kd3 { And again. } Kb2 ( 6... a3 { Reaching the important Position 5.14 on White's turn ! } 7. Rb8 $18 ) 
7. Rb8 Ka2 8. Kc2 $18 ) 
2. Kf7 a5 $1 3. Ke6 a4 $1 { The pawn advances as the King holds. } 4. Ke5 a3 5. Ke4 ( 5. Ra8 { Is another attempt. } Kb4 6. Kd4 Kb3 $1 7. Kd3 { See Ending 28. } Kb2 $1 ( 7. Ke4 a2 8. Rb8 Ka3 9. Kc2 $1 a1=N 10. Kc3 $18 ) 
8. Kd2 a2 9. Rb8 Ka1 $11 ) 
Kc4 ( 5... a2 $4 6. Ra8 ) 
6. Rc8+ Kb3 7. Kd3 { See Ending 28. } Kb2 $1 ( 7... a2 8. Rb8 Ka3 9. Kc2 $18 ) 
8. Rb8+ Kc1 $1 9. Kc3 a2 10. Ra8 Kb1 11. Rb8+ Kc1 $11 ( 11... Ka1 $2 12. Re8 $18 ) 

