 { Black (to move) is down two pawns, but he can recover both of them. Is this enough to save him ? } 1... Kd6 $1 ( 1... Rxb4 $2 { Black recovers both pawns just to reach a lost endgame. It was far better to bring the King closer. } 2. Kf5 Rxh4 3. Re4 Rh8 4. Rd4 Kc5 5. Rd3 Ra8 6. f4 Ra4 7. Rd7 Kc6 8. Rf7 Kd6 9. Rxf6 Ke7 10. Kg6 $1 { The poor positioning of the Black pieces prevents them from achieving Philidor (Ending 52) (or Kling & Horwitz) (Ending 56) positions } Ra1 11. Rf7 $1 Ke8 12. f5 Rg1 13. Kf6 Rf1 { The position is similar to 'Kling & Horwitz', but in the text of Ending 56 we read that against a Bishop's pawn, the King must be on the short side. Here, that is not possible. } 14. Ra7 Rf2 15. Ra8 Kd7 16. Rf8 $1 Rh2 17. Kg7 Rg2 18. Kf7 Ra2 19. f6 Ra7 20. Re8 Ra6 21. Re1 { And the Lucena position Ending 53 will be achieved. Black resigned and Russia, Karjakin, beat Ukraine, Volokitin, in the 2012 Chess Olympiad. } ) 
2. Ra7 ( 2. Rh7 Ke6 $11 ) 
Rxb4+ 3. Kf5 Rxh4 4. f4 Rh6 $1 5. Rf7 Rh4 6. Rxf6+ Ke7 $11 { With the Black Rook on h4, White cannot make progress. } 
